# Hand-written expected files (plan 5.17.4, M24). A test that sets
# REDEFINE: %{msil_expected_rule} = <os>[,<os>]: <reason> has a hand-written
# expected file for each named OS; the native-oracle commands of every
# composite (msil-prog.py --mode native and the native run) take
# --expected-rule '%{msil_expected_rule}', and when the rule names the
# oracle's OS each prints 'native oracle skipped (hand-written expected
# file): <reason>' and exits 0, while every MSIL run still compares with the
# file; update-expected.py neither builds nor writes for a named OS and logs
# 'rule: <file> (<reason>)', or 'MISSING: ...' when the file does not exist.
#
# (1) msil-run.py --native --expected-rule, on a stand-in native program
# (Inputs/fake-native-44.il, a .NET Framework executable that exits with 44
# when it gets no arguments): the oracle's OS is --expect-os (%msil_exec
# passes windows); a rule that names it, alone or with the other OS, skips
# the run and its comparison (a wrong expected file passes, nothing is
# written to --out); a rule for the other OS and the empty rule compare as
# before; a malformed rule is a usage error (status 2) that names what is
# wrong, and so is --expected-rule without --native.
# (2) msil-prog.py --mode native --expected-rule: a rule for the triple's OS
# prints the skip line and leaves --workdir untouched; the skip comes only
# after the usage checks; a malformed rule is a usage error; a rule for the
# other OS builds as before (with the real clang where the native oracle is
# available); --mode msil ignores the rule.
# (3) The composite, in a nested lit with -a of prog/Inputs/nested-rule
# (msil-prog.py with the stand-ins of prog/Inputs/prog-cc.py, whose native
# program exits with one more than the MSIL program: prog-native): rule.c
# passes only through its rule, and both native steps print the skip line;
# norule.c, the same test without the rule, fails at its native run;
# rule-msil-wrong.c fails at its MSIL run although its rule names both OSes;
# rule-other.c, whose rule names only linux, fails at its native run.
# (4) update-expected.py on copies of rule.c and rule-other.c: 'rule:' with
# --check and without (the hand-written file stays as it is), 'MISSING:'
# without the file (status 1, nothing written), and for rule-other.c the
# native build and comparison as for any test (the stand-in compiler of (1)
# copies itself, so the native transcript is [exit 44]: MISMATCH). Writing
# never replaces rule-other.expected, the hand-written Linux file (there is
# no rule-other.linux.expected): it writes rule-other.windows.expected
# instead ('wrote <file> (rule-other.expected is the hand-written linux
# expected file)'), which --check then reads; norule.c, which has no rule,
# gets its norule.expected replaced. A malformed rule (rule-bad.c), and a
# %{msil_oracle_flags} value with a word other than --hosted, the only one
# the oracle knows (flags-bad.c), fail the test before anything is built:
# the reason on stderr, then 'FAIL (test file): <test>' (status 1, nothing
# written); flags.c, which differs from flags-bad.c only by that word, gets
# its rule's line (both rules name windows, so neither test is built).
# Windows only: the stand-in native programs are .NET Framework executables,
# which %msil_exec runs locally only under the Windows triple (the WSL legs,
# whose %msil_exec passes --runner wsl --expect-os linux, leave this test out).
# REQUIRES: system-windows, msil-triple-windows, msil-netfx

